{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BCDNZVZH31iWrDxjHrb7VK","type":"tool_result","content":"16058:declare function createApiClient(baseUrl: string, options?: ApiClientOptions): openapi_fetch.Client<paths, `${string}/${string}`>;\n16536:    baseUrl?: string;\n---\n16534:interface PollarClientConfig {\n16535-    stellarNetwork?: StellarNetwork;\n16536-    baseUrl?: string;\n16537-    apiKey: string;\n16538-    /**\n16539-     * Pluggable storage. Defaults to `defaultStorage()` on web (localStorage\n16540-     * with memory fallback). On RN you must inject one of the adapters from\n16541-     * `@pollar/core/adapters/expo` or `@pollar/core/adapters/react-native-keychain`.\n16542-     */\n16543-    storage?: Storage;\n16544-    /**\n16545-     * Max time (ms) a single SDK HTTP attempt waits before aborting and rejecting\n16546-     * with a {@link PollarNetworkError} (`code: 'SDK_NETWORK_TIMEOUT'`).\n16547-     *\n16548-     * `fetch` has no timeout of its own, so without this a transient connection\n16549-     * stall (e.g. a dropped TCP SYN on a flaky mobile network at cold start) hangs\n16550-     * the request forever - it neither resolves nor rejects, trapping any caller\n16551-     * that `await`s it (a returning user stuck on the splash screen). Bounding it\n16552-     * lets the request fail fast so the caller can recover (retry, or fall back to\n16553-     * a cached token).\n16554-     *\n16555-     * Defaults to `10000` (10s). Set `0` to disable (NOT recommended - restores\n16556-     * the unbounded-hang behavior).\n16557-     */\n16558-    requestTimeoutMs?: number;\n16559-    /**\n16560-     * Per-request timeout (ms) applied to the submit-family tx calls\n16561-     * (`submitTx`, `signAndSubmitTx`, `buildAndSignAndSubmitTx`) instead of\n16562-     * {@link requestTimeoutMs}. These endpoints do real server-side work\n16563-     * (embedded build + sign via wallet-service, then a network submit) that can\n16564-     * take several seconds, so the default 10s request timeout is too tight and\n16565-     * would cut a submit that is actually succeeding. Everything else still uses\n16566-     * `requestTimeoutMs`.\n16567-     *\n16568-     * Defaults to `30000` (30s).\n16569-     */\n16570-    submitTimeoutMs?: number;\n16571-    /**\n16572-     * Per-request timeout (ms) for the final `POST /auth/login`, instead of\n16573-     * {@link requestTimeoutMs}.\n16574-     *\n16575-     * That call is where a login does its real server-side work - minting tokens,\n16576-     * resolving the wallet, and, for an app whose account creation runs inline,\n16577-     * waiting on the Stellar network. The 10s default that protects every other\n16578-     * request is far too tight for it: a congested network has pushed it past a\n16579-     * minute, and the client aborting mid-flight does not stop the server, so the\n16580-     * work completed with nobody left to receive the tokens.\n16581-     *\n16582-     * Defaults to `45000` (45s). It is a backstop, not a fix - an app whose\n16583-     * account creation is asynchronous returns in a couple of seconds.\n16584-     */\n16585-    loginTimeoutMs?: number;\n16586-    /**\n16587-     * Automatic retry with backoff for idempotent, transient-failure SDK HTTP\n16588-     * (token refresh + GETs), to absorb a single dropped request before surfacing\n16589-     * an error. Only transport failures retry; an HTTP response is never retried.\n16590-     * Defaults to `{ attempts: 2, baseDelayMs: 300 }`. See {@link PollarRetryConfig}.\n16591-     */\n16592-    retry?: PollarRetryConfig;\n16593-    /**\n16594-     * Pluggable DPoP key manager. Defaults to `defaultKeyManager(storage,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5365cfbc-8bd5-448f-aee4-5834d27e5280","uuid":"974ef05c-74c2-4e9e-a060-707cd9c24b86","timestamp":"2026-11-09T20:46:05.065Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"16058:declare function createApiClient(baseUrl: string, options